<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kiel mutiny</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as naval mutiny at Kiel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a> mutiny was a revolt by sailors of the German High Seas Fleet in 1918, sparked by orders for a final naval sortie against the British at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. The unrest spread rapidly to other cities, giving rise to workers' and soldiers' councils. It triggered the German Revolution that led to the abdication of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> and the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kiel-mutiny.jpg" alt="Kiel mutiny" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-J0908-0600-002,_Novemberrevolution,_Matrosenaufstand.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jutland">Battle of Jutland</a> in 1916 the High Seas Fleet lay mostly at anchor, and as experienced officers left for submarines and light vessels, discipline on the capital ships suffered. Unrest began in the summer of 1917: in August most of the 600-man crew of SMS Prinzregent Luitpold walked off in support of arrested comrades, and two ringleaders were executed. Secret sailors' councils formed on several ships.</p><p>In October 1918 the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-maximilian-of-baden">Prince Maximilian of Baden</a> asked President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a> to mediate an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> and ended submarine warfare. Admiral Scheer, Chief of the Admiralty Staff, then on his own authority had Admiral Hipper, commander of the High Seas Fleet, prepare the main fleet for an attack on the British. The historian Michael Epkenhans calls it an admirals' rebellion, planned against the political leadership.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On the night of 30 to 31 October sailors of III Battle Squadron in the Schillig Roads off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelmshaven">Wilhelmshaven</a> refused to weigh anchor, with outright mutiny on Thüringen and Helgoland. The command dropped the attack and sent the squadron to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a>, where Vice Admiral Hugo Kraft had 47 sailors of the Markgraf and nearly 150 other mutineers arrested. Sailors and workers met to demand their release, and on 3 November, led by Karl Artelt and Lothar Popp of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/independent-social-democratic-party-of-germany">USPD</a>, several thousand marched on the military prison under the slogan peace and bread. A patrol fired into the crowd, killing seven and seriously wounding 29.</p><p>On 4 November troops sent to reinforce the garrison joined the demonstrators, Admiral Wilhelm Souchon freed the prisoners, and Artelt organised the first soldiers' council. By that evening about 40,000 sailors, soldiers and workers held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a>. They presented the Social Democrat Gustav Noske, sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, with fourteen demands, among them freedom of speech and the press and that the fleet never sail. They elected Noske chairman of the council, and on 5 November the red flag flew on the ships in the harbour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Detachments of sailors spread from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a> to the major cities and met almost no resistance. By 7 November councils held the larger coastal cities, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> am Main, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, where King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-iii-of-bavaria">Ludwig III of Bavaria</a> became the first German prince to fall. The revolution reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on 9 November, and the other dynastic rulers abdicated by the end of the month. After street battles in January 1919, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> was established in August 1919.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kiel mutiny” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the failure of the Spring Offensive of 1918, the <b>naval mutiny at Kiel</b> in early November triggered the November Revolution.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
